# Introducing User-Centered Design

The modern site from the last level didn't just look nicer, its nav bar, hero section, and gallery cards were arranged around a specific goal: *making it easy for a visitor to find what they came for*. 

That's the shift worth naming for your students. Moving from a cluttered, text-heavy page to a clean, navigable one isn't a matter of taste, it's a stronger focus on the person actually using the site.

<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; margin: 20px 0;">

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
<p>📖 <b>User Experience (UX)</b> is the overall experience a person has using a product or service. Good UX means it's easy to use, efficient, and even enjoyable, not just functional.</p>
</div>

  <div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px;">
    <p>📖 <b>Human-Centered Design</b> is the practice of starting from the needs of the end user, rather than starting from what's easiest to build or what looks impressive to a designer.</p>
  </div>

</div>

---

<h2>💼 Career Connection: UX Designer</h2>

<div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/9627ad323ccfe8be5ca9fcebe95e0aba-lesson6-panel4.png" alt="A UX designer's process, showing wireframe sketches and a user flow diagram" style="width: 30%; max-width: 220px; border-radius: 10px;">
  
  <p style="flex: 1;">A User Experience (UX) Designer focuses on making digital products easy, enjoyable, and meaningful to use. They research what users actually need, test early drafts, and shape layouts that improve how people interact with a site or app before a single line of code gets written.</p>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #222222; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
<strong>💡 Teacher tip:</strong> you'll see career connections like this throughout the unit. Use them to help students see how the skills they're learning, problem-solving, design thinking, and eventually coding, connect to real jobs in tech.
</div>


